\title{snakeplot: Serpentine Plots for Long Timeline, Sequential and Survey Data}
\description{
\pkg{snakeplot} draws serpentine ("snake") plots: displays in which a long
ordered axis (days, time points, positions in a sequence or items of a
questionnaire) is folded into parallel bands joined by U-turns. The whole
axis fits in one panel, its order is kept, and neighbouring bands can be
compared directly. All plots are drawn with base R graphics.

The package covers three kinds of data: event times and continuous
measurements within days, categorical state sequences, and responses to
rating items, including experience-sampling data collected several times
a day. Each plot function draws on the current graphics device; the
value it returns invisibly is described on its help page.
}
\details{
Every plot shares the same layout. Bands are stacked from top to bottom;
with \code{flow = "snake"} they alternate direction so that the axis runs on
through each U-turn, and with \code{flow = "natural"} every band reads in the
same direction, like lines of text. \code{start_from} chooses the side of the
first band. Most functions default to \code{"snake"}; \code{\link[=sequence_snake]{sequence_snake()}} and
\code{\link[=timeline_snake]{timeline_snake()}} default to \code{"natural"}.

A serpentine changes the reading of distances. Positions at the ends of
adjacent bands are close on the page but far apart on the axis. In
\code{\link[=activity_snake]{activity_snake()}} and \code{\link[=line_snake]{line_snake()}}, points above one another in
adjacent bands share a time of day only when the bands read in the same
direction (\code{flow = "natural"}). Band labels and U-turn labels give the
position on the axis.
}
\section{Time within days}{

\code{\link[=activity_snake]{activity_snake()}} shows event times, one band per day, as ticks or
blocks of their duration. \code{\link[=line_snake]{line_snake()}} draws a continuous series as a
line through the days. \code{\link[=facet_snake]{facet_snake()}} draws one panel per group.
}

\section{Sequences}{

\code{\link[=sequence_snake]{sequence_snake()}} shows one long categorical sequence as colored blocks
along the path. \code{\link[=timeline_snake]{timeline_snake()}} builds a monthly sequence from phases
with start and end dates, such as the positions in a career.
\code{\link[=multi_snake]{multi_snake()}} shows many sequences as a sequence index or state
distribution display (Gabadinho et al., 2011).
}

\section{Survey and experience-sampling data}{

\code{\link[=survey_snake]{survey_snake()}} shows every response to each item as a tick, with the
item mean, optional correlations between adjacent items, and a mode for
experience-sampling data with one band per day. \code{\link[=survey_sequence]{survey_sequence()}} and
\code{\link[=sequential_dist]{sequential_dist()}} show one 100\% stacked bar per item, with diverging
or single-hue colors (see Heiberger & Robbins, 2014, on stacked bars for
rating scales). \code{\link[=snake_palette]{snake_palette()}} and \link{snake_palettes} provide colors for
response levels.
}

\section{Data}{

\link{ema_emotions}, \link{student_survey} and \link{ema_beeps} come from a 14-day
experience-sampling study of university students (Neubauer & Schmiedek,
2024).
}

